(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-cardiac-physiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiac Physiology — Physiology Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is the sinoatrial (SA) node the natural pacemaker of the heart?","choices":["It has the slowest rate of automatic discharge among cardiac tissues, at 40-60/min, compared to the A-V node (70-80/min) and Purkinje fibers (90-100/min)","It is the only tissue in the heart capable of generating an action potential, since the A-V node and Purkinje fibers lack automaticity entirely","The SA node has no automatic discharge at all, requiring continuous excitatory input from the vagus nerve to trigger each heartbeat","It has the highest (fastest) rate of automatic discharge among cardiac tissues, at 70-80/min, compared to the A-V node (40-60/min) and Purkinje fibers (15-40/min)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The SA node is the pacemaker of the heart because it has the highest (fastest) rate of automatic discharge — normally 70-80/min — compared to the A-V node (40-60/min) and Purkinje fibers (15-40/min), so it triggers the next heartbeat before other tissues can reach their own threshold. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What ionic mechanism causes the SA node's resting membrane potential to gradually rise toward threshold?","choices":["The SA node has a stable resting potential with no drift, remaining fixed at -90 mV between each heartbeat","A constant leak of Na+ into the cell, since the membrane sits at -55 to -60 mV where fast Na+ channels are inactivated","A constant leak of Cl- into the cell, since the membrane sits at -90 mV where chloride channels remain persistently open","A constant leak of K+ out of the cell, since the membrane sits at -70 mV where potassium conductance is unusually high"],"answer":1,"exp":"The SA node membrane leaks Na+ continuously, and because its resting potential (-55 to -60 mV) is less negative than in ventricular muscle, fast Na+ channels are inactivated at this voltage — the constant Na+ leak instead gradually raises the membrane potential toward threshold. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What ionic events characterize phase 4 (slow depolarization) and phase 0 (rapid depolarization) of the SA node action potential?","choices":["Both phases involve only fast Na+ channels, exactly as seen in ventricular muscle action potentials","Phase 4: inward Na+ \"funny\" current; phase 0: inward Na+/Ca2+ current via slow (L-type) Ca2+ channels","Phase 0 involves only potassium efflux through delayed rectifier channels, without any depolarizing current","Phase 4: outward K+ current; phase 0: inward Cl- current through ligand-gated chloride channels"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the SA node, phase 4 (slow depolarization) is driven by an inward Na+ \"funny\" current through special channels, while phase 0 (rapid depolarization/the actual action potential upstroke) is driven by inward Na+/Ca2+ current through slow Na+-Ca2+ (L-type Ca2+) channels — fast Na+ channels are not involved because they're inactivated at the SA node's less-negative resting potential. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What ionic events produce phase 3 (repolarization) of the SA node action potential?","choices":["Inactivation of the slow Na+-Ca2+ channels combined with an outward K+ current","A sudden decrease in K+ conductance combined with sustained inward Na+ current","Opening of fast Na+ channels combined with rapid closure of Ca2+ channels","Continued inward Ca2+ current with progressively increasing K+ conductance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Phase 3 (repolarization) of the SA node action potential results from inactivation of the slow Na+-Ca2+ channels combined with an outward K+ current, returning the membrane potential to about -55 mV. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the function of the internodal pathways, and which one carries impulses specifically to the left atrium?","choices":["The posterior internodal pathway carries impulses to the left atrium, while the anterior band supplies only the SA node region","They exist only in diseased hearts, forming as compensatory conduction routes after myocardial infarction","They only conduct impulses within the ventricles, connecting the A-V bundle branches to the Purkinje network","They transmit the cardiac impulse throughout the atria; the anterior interatrial band carries impulses to the left atrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior, middle, and posterior internodal pathways transmit the cardiac impulse throughout the atria from the SA node; the anterior interatrial band specifically carries impulses to the left atrium. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Where does most of the delay in cardiac impulse conduction occur, and how long is that delay?","choices":["There is no measurable delay anywhere in the conduction system between the SA node and ventricles","In the atrial muscle itself, about 0.08 seconds, with no additional delay elsewhere","In the Purkinje fibers, about 0.03 seconds, with no delay in the A-V node itself","In the A-V node, about 0.09 seconds (with an additional 0.04 seconds in the A-V bundle)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Most of the conduction delay occurs in the A-V node (about 0.09 seconds), with additional delay in the A-V bundle (about 0.04 seconds) — this delay allows the atria to finish contracting and empty into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the only normal conducting pathway between the atria and ventricles?","choices":["Direct myocardial contact between atrial and ventricular muscle","The internodal pathways alone","The A-V node and the (left and right) A-V bundles","The coronary sinus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Because a fibrous insulator separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia, the only conducting pathway between them is the A-V node and the left and right A-V bundles; transmission time between the A-V bundles and the last ventricular fibers is about 0.06 seconds (corresponding to the QRS duration). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How does the conduction velocity of the Purkinje system compare to that of the A-V node?","choices":["Neither structure conducts electrical impulses","Both conduct at identical velocity","Purkinje system is slow (0.2-0.5 m/s); A-V node is fast (2-4 m/s)","Purkinje system is fast (2-4 m/s); A-V node is slow (0.2-0.5 m/s)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Purkinje system conducts rapidly (2-4 m/s) due to fast conduction and many gap junctions at intercalated disks, while the A-V node conducts slowly (0.2-0.5 m/s) — this slow A-V conduction is what allows the atria to finish contracting before ventricular excitation, despite the fast Purkinje distribution once past the node. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What ionic mechanism underlies the parasympathetic (vagal) slowing of heart rate?","choices":["Acetylcholine decreases K+ permeability while increasing Na+ permeability, depolarizing the SA node and accelerating its rate of self-excitation","Acetylcholine causes hyperpolarization due to increased K+ permeability, decreasing SA node discharge and A-V fiber excitability, slowing heart rate (and potentially causing ventricular escape)","Acetylcholine has no effect on membrane potential, acting instead only on contractile proteins within atrial and ventricular myocytes directly","Vagal stimulation increases Na+ and Ca2+ permeability like sympathetic stimulation, producing a more positive resting potential and faster discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic (vagal) nerves release acetylcholine at the SA node and A-V junctional fibers, causing hyperpolarization via increased K+ permeability; this decreases SA node discharge rate and A-V fiber excitability, slowing heart rate and potentially causing a temporary pause with ventricular escape. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What ionic mechanism underlies the sympathetic acceleration of heart rate?","choices":["Norepinephrine increases K+ permeability, hyperpolarizing the SA node and slowing its rate of spontaneous discharge considerably","Norepinephrine increases permeability to Na+ and Ca2+, producing a more positive resting potential that accelerates self-excitation and increases A-V fiber excitability","Norepinephrine has no effect on ion permeability, acting instead solely through increased myocardial contractility via cyclic AMP","Sympathetic stimulation slows the rate of conduction through the A-V node while leaving the SA node's discharge rate completely unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Norepinephrine released by sympathetic nerves increases membrane permeability to Na+ and Ca2+, producing a more positive resting potential that accelerates self-excitation of the SA node and increases the rate of conduction and excitability of A-V fibers. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What do the terms \"chronotropic,\" \"dromotropic,\" and \"inotropic\" refer to regarding autonomic effects on the heart?","choices":["These terms apply only to the parasympathetic system","Chronotropic = contractility; dromotropic = rate; inotropic = conduction velocity","Chronotropic = rate; dromotropic = conduction velocity; inotropic = contractility","All three terms refer to the same effect on the heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"\"Chronotropic\" refers to effects on heart rate, \"dromotropic\" to effects on conduction velocity, and \"inotropic\" to effects on contractility; sympathetic stimulation (via norepinephrine/ADRB1) produces positive effects on all three, while parasympathetic stimulation (via acetylcholine/mAChR) produces negative effects on all three. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which adrenergic receptor mediates the sympathetic nervous system's effects on heart rate and contractility?","choices":["Beta-2 receptor exclusively","Alpha-1 receptor","Muscarinic receptor","Beta-1 receptor (ADRB1)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation of the heart acts through the beta-1 adrenergic receptor (ADRB1), causing increased heart rate, increased rate of conduction, increased contractility, and increased cardiac output. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which receptor mediates the parasympathetic nervous system's effects on the heart?","choices":["Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor (mAChR)","Beta-1 adrenergic receptor","Nicotinic acetylcholine receptor exclusively","Alpha-2 adrenergic receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parasympathetic stimulation of the heart acts through the muscarinic acetylcholine receptor (mAChR), causing decreased heart rate, decreased rate of conduction, decreased contractility, and decreased cardiac output. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are the three main types of ion channels in cardiac muscle, and which type is NOT involved in generating the SA node action potential?","choices":["Only K+ channels exist in cardiac muscle, with depolarization occurring passively via extracellular Ca2+ diffusion alone","Fast Na+, slow Na+-Ca2+, and K+ channels — fast Na+ channels are not involved in SA node APs since they are inactivated at its less-negative resting potential","Only fast Na+ channels generate the SA node action potential, exactly as in ventricular muscle and skeletal muscle fibers","All three channel types are equally involved in every cardiac cell type, with no functional difference between the SA node and ventricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cardiac muscle has three main ion channel types — fast Na+ channels, slow Na+-Ca2+ (L-type Ca2+) channels, and K+ channels — but fast Na+ channels are inactivated at the SA node's relatively depolarized resting potential and are therefore not involved in generating its action potential (unlike in ventricular muscle). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the QRS complex duration on an EKG correspond to physiologically?","choices":["The transmission time between the A-V bundles and the last of the ventricular fibers, about 0.06 seconds","The duration of atrial repolarization, which is normally obscured by the QRS complex itself","The delay within the A-V node alone, lasting approximately 0.09 seconds before ventricular activation","The time for the SA node to discharge and initiate the impulse, roughly 0.02 seconds"],"answer":0,"exp":"The QRS complex duration corresponds to the transmission time between the A-V bundles and the last of the ventricular fibers to depolarize, approximately 0.06 seconds. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Where does the electrical activity generating the P wave begin, and in what direction does it spread?","choices":["The P wave has no defined origin, arising simultaneously and randomly throughout both atria","It begins at the A-V node and spreads toward the SA node, giving a negative vector in leads I, II, and III","It begins in the ventricles and spreads retrograde to the atria, giving an inverted P wave morphology","It begins at the sinus (SA) node and spreads toward the A-V node, giving a positive vector in leads I, II, and III"],"answer":3,"exp":"The P wave (representing atrial depolarization) begins at the sinus node and spreads toward the A-V node, producing a positive deflection in leads I, II, and III. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why is the atrial T wave (atrial repolarization) not visible on a standard EKG?","choices":["It is obscured by the much larger QRS complex, which occurs at approximately the same time","Atrial repolarization produces no detectable signal, since only depolarization generates a measurable current","Atria do not repolarize between beats, remaining continuously depolarized throughout the cardiac cycle","It occurs several seconds after the P wave, well after the QRS complex has already ended"],"answer":0,"exp":"The atrial T wave (representing atrial repolarization) cannot be seen on a standard EKG because it is obscured by the QRS complex, which occurs around the same time; because atrial depolarization is slower than ventricular depolarization, the first area to depolarize is also the first to repolarize, producing a wave that would be negative in leads I, II, and III if it were visible. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is \"vagal tone,\" and what happens to heart rate if the vagus nerves are severed?","choices":["Severing the vagus nerves would decrease heart rate further, since vagal tone normally accelerates SA node discharge above its intrinsic rate","The baseline background parasympathetic activity that keeps resting heart rate lower than the SA node's intrinsic rate; severing the vagus nerves increases heart rate","A term with no real physiological meaning, since parasympathetic input to the heart is negligible at rest under normal conditions","Vagal tone only affects the ventricles, not the SA node, slowing conduction through the Purkinje fiber network exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vagal tone is the baseline (background) level of parasympathetic activity continuously restraining heart rate below the SA node's intrinsic discharge rate; severing the vagus nerves removes this restraint and increases heart rate, similar to the effect of blocking ACh receptors with atropine. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What happens to heart rate when acetylcholine receptors are blocked with atropine?","choices":["Heart rate is completely unaffected, since atropine acts only on nicotinic receptors in the adrenal medulla","Atropine has no effect on the cardiovascular system, acting exclusively on smooth muscle within the gastrointestinal tract","Heart rate decreases further, since atropine potentiates rather than blocks the vagal slowing effect on the SA node","Heart rate increases, since atropine blocks the muscarinic receptors mediating the vagal slowing effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Blocking acetylcholine (muscarinic) receptors with atropine removes the vagal restraint on the SA node, causing heart rate to increase (unmasking the SA node's faster intrinsic discharge rate). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"In a normal heart, from what general anatomic direction does the mean electrical vector of the partially depolarized heart typically point?","choices":["From base to apex (and top to bottom, back to front, right to left)","From left to right only (with no vertical or anteroposterior component)","From apex to base (and bottom to top, front to back)","The mean vector changes direction randomly with each individual heartbeat"],"answer":0,"exp":"In a normally depolarizing heart, the mean electrical vector typically points from base to apex (and correspondingly from top to bottom, back to front, and right to left), reflecting the normal sequence of ventricular depolarization. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does the septum and endocardium show a longer contraction duration compared to the outer ventricular surface?","choices":["The septum/endocardium is the first area to depolarize and the last to repolarize, giving it a longer action potential/contraction duration; the outer ventricular surface is the last to depolarize but the first to repolarize","The outer ventricular surface is the first area to depolarize and the last to repolarize, giving it a longer contraction duration than the septum, which depolarizes last but repolarizes first","Depolarization and repolarization occur simultaneously everywhere in the ventricle at once, so contraction duration is identical throughout both the septum and the outer wall","The septum never depolarizes at all, remaining electrically silent throughout the entire cardiac cycle while only the outer ventricular wall depolarizes and contracts normally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The septum and endocardium are the first areas to depolarize but the last to repolarize, giving them a longer contraction duration; conversely, the outer surface of the ventricles is the last to depolarize but the first to repolarize. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the functional purpose of the delay at the A-V node?","choices":["To speed up conduction to the ventricles, ensuring atrial and ventricular contraction occur simultaneously","To allow the atria to finish contracting and empty their contents into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins","The A-V node delay serves no functional purpose, existing only as a vestigial remnant of embryonic conduction tissue","To prevent the atria from ever contracting, redirecting all electrical activity directly to the ventricles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The A-V node delay (about 0.09 seconds, the largest component of AV conduction delay) allows the atria to finish contracting and empty their blood into the ventricles before ventricular contraction begins, optimizing ventricular filling. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the overall purpose of the cardiac conduction system?","choices":["To produce the heart sounds heard on auscultation, generated directly by electrical current passing through cardiac tissue","To regulate blood pressure independent of the heart, acting through direct innervation of peripheral arteriolar smooth muscle","To generate the mechanical force of contraction directly, bypassing the need for excitation-contraction coupling in myocytes","To generate and rapidly distribute the electrical impulse that triggers coordinated atrial and then ventricular contraction, with an appropriate delay between the two"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cardiac conduction system (SA node, internodal pathways, A-V node, A-V bundle, and Purkinje fibers) generates and distributes the electrical impulse that triggers coordinated contraction — first of the atria, then (after an appropriate A-V nodal delay) of the ventricles. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the anatomic path that Purkinje fibers follow to reach ventricular muscle?","choices":["Purkinje fibers do not connect to the A-V node at all","From the ventricular apex back up to the atria","Directly from the SA node to the ventricular apex, bypassing the A-V node entirely","From the A-V node, through the A-V bundle, into the ventricles"],"answer":3,"exp":"Purkinje fibers lead from the A-V node, through the A-V bundle, into the ventricles, providing fast conduction (via many gap junctions at intercalated disks) to rapidly distribute the impulse throughout the ventricular myocardium. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Rank the following from fastest to slowest normal intrinsic discharge rate: SA node, A-V node, Purkinje fibers.","choices":["Purkinje fibers > A-V node > SA node","SA node > A-V node > Purkinje fibers","All three discharge at identical rates","A-V node > SA node > Purkinje fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"From fastest to slowest intrinsic discharge rate: SA node (70-80/min) > A-V node (40-60/min) > Purkinje fibers (15-40/min) — this hierarchy is why the SA node normally sets the pace, but lower structures can take over (as an \"escape\" rhythm) if the SA node fails. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, in which direction does conduction occur through the A-V bundles?","choices":["Bidirectionally with equal ease in both directions","Only from ventricles to atria","Normally one-way, from atria to ventricles","The A-V bundles do not conduct impulses at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normally, conduction through the A-V bundles is one-way, from the atria to the ventricles, ensuring that the atria always contract before the ventricles under physiological conditions. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Besides increasing heart rate, what other cardiac effects does sympathetic stimulation via norepinephrine produce?","choices":["No measurable effect on contractility, conduction, or heart rate at physiological doses","Increased rate of conduction and increased contractility in atria and ventricles","Decreased cardiac output despite an increase in heart rate and contractility","Decreased contractility with unchanged conduction velocity throughout the atria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation via norepinephrine increases sinus node discharge (rate), increases the rate of conduction of the impulse, and increases the force of contraction in both the atria and ventricles. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the \"funny current\" referred to in SA node phase 4 depolarization?","choices":["A current with no known ionic basis, thought to arise instead from mechanical stretch of the pacemaker cell membrane","An outward potassium current that drives the slow, spontaneous phase 4 repolarization of pacemaker cells","An outward chloride current that only occurs in ventricular muscle during the plateau phase","An inward Na+ current through special channels that drives the slow, spontaneous phase 4 depolarization of pacemaker cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"The \"funny current\" is an inward Na+ current through special channels that drives the slow, spontaneous phase 4 depolarization characteristic of the SA node and other pacemaker tissue, gradually bringing the membrane potential toward threshold. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is \"ventricular escape,\" in the context of strong vagal stimulation?","choices":["A state of complete, permanent cardiac arrest that occurs whenever vagal stimulation exceeds a critical threshold of intensity","An increase in SA node discharge rate that occurs as a reflexive compensatory response to strong, sustained vagal stimulation","A rhythm where, if strong vagal stimulation temporarily stops SA/AV conduction, a lower pacemaker (e.g., Purkinje fibers) takes over to maintain some ventricular contraction","A dangerous, unpredictable arrhythmia that arises spontaneously and has no relationship whatsoever to vagal or sympathetic stimulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"If strong vagal (parasympathetic) stimulation temporarily halts impulse transmission from the SA/AV node, a lower intrinsic pacemaker (such as the Purkinje fibers) can take over and maintain some ventricular contraction — this is called ventricular escape. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How does the speed of atrial depolarization compare to ventricular depolarization, and what is the functional consequence for the atrial T wave?","choices":["Atrial depolarization is faster than ventricular depolarization, so the first area to depolarize is the last to repolarize, producing a positive wave in leads I, II, and III","Atrial and ventricular depolarization occur at identical speeds, so their respective repolarization waves appear as mirror images of one another on the EKG tracing","Atrial depolarization does not produce any detectable repolarization wave at all, since the atrial muscle mass is simply too small to generate a measurable one","Atrial depolarization is slower than ventricular depolarization, so the first atrial area to depolarize is also the first to repolarize, producing what would be a negative wave in leads I, II, and III"],"answer":3,"exp":"Atrial depolarization is slower than ventricular depolarization; because of this, the first atrial area to depolarize is also the first to repolarize (unlike the ventricles, where the sequence reverses), which would produce a negative deflection in leads I, II, and III if it weren't obscured by the QRS complex. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 55"}]};
